based on your submissions our parent  company nexstar has chosen its top four  choices of remarkable women of nevada  and today we introduce you to maggie  carlton who's the longest-serving female  legislator in our state  i grew up in the show me state but i'm a  nevadan by choice and that's where my  heart lies  maggie carlton moved to the east part of  las vegas in 1990 securing a job as a  coffee waitress in treasure island  where she took a big interest in the  culinary union and politics  a lot of it had to do with being a  working mom  who wanted to represent folks from the  perspective of a working mom we walked  the frontier picket line  on fridays and i took my daughters down  there to walk the picket line maggie was  chosen as an active member of the  culinary  union to introduce then president bill  clinton the president  of the united states and it wasn't long  before this waitress with a high school  diploma  became the first labor activist to be  elected to the nevada state  senate a couple of the more seasoned  legislators back then tell the story  that  they they had no idea where this this  young waitress came from and  you know they thought i was going to  show up in a motorcycle jacket in  stiletto heels or something i mean they  were  they were just joking but you know i i  was an unknown i came out of  out of nowhere this unknown at the time  would later become the ultimate  stereotype buster representing the  little guy across our state on very  important matters  it was very nerve-wracking because i had  never done  anything like that before the major  challenges for her came with balancing  family life  if you stop and think about it i've done  this for 22 years and they're 34 and 35  years old so  it's been most of their lives they've  had a mom that's in the legislature so  that's that's been one of the biggest  challenges is is balancing that and  always making sure that i was  there for them you know as much as i  could be here's a snippet from inside  edition when maggie a senator at the  time  and still a cafe waitress was profiled  on her political career  do you think you'd vote for her in 2011  maggie was elected to the nevada  assembly  and became the longest serving woman  legislature in nevada's history  now being able to help the next  generation that's coming up  she has been chair of the ways and means  committee since 2013.  one of her biggest accomplishments took  10 years of her life to complete  tackling surprise hospital billing if  you're out on the soccer field and your  your daughter falls and and breaks the  elbow or whatever  you don't think about what hospitals and  network you just up you take them to the  closest hospital you walk in the  emergency room door  and you get them treatment well a lot of  times what would happen after that is  the surprise bill would come later and  now hospitals and insurance companies  negotiate on your behalf  while these big issues are certainly  important maggie finds the littler ones  most satisfying when somebody calls you  and there's  there's a street light out on their  street or they're having problems with  the sidewalk or their child needed a  zone variance  or there was an issue with the neighbor  the fact that you could reach out and  help people  do things for their everyday life it  was very it's very very gratifying as  for being nominated as a remarkable  woman of nevada i was just so surprised  and emotional about the letter submitted  on her behalf  written by her husband it was very  important  you know because you know sometimes  after 40 years of marriage you don't  appreciate each other as much or share  as much  you just kind of you know we're there  for each other but  the fact that he took the time to do  that means a lot  maggie will continue her work as  executive director with non-profit you  land the united labor agency of nevada  in 2022 after 24 years of public service  she says she'll be turned out and she  says it'll be tough to get politics out  of her blood  but she adds one good thing about term  limits is that you're always getting new  people and she believes that's a good  thing for the state of nevada  as it's constantly changing
 

